# Notes on the Stroke License

The Stroke License is based on the MMF License, which Noah Nuebling wrote for
Mac Mouse Fix (https://github.com/noah-nuebling/mac-mouse-fix/blob/master/License).
The MMF License is itself based on the MIT and DBAD licenses.

I wrote it with two goals in mind:

1. To stop people from publishing apps built from Stroke that contain malware,
   or that look just like Stroke except that when you buy a license, someone
   other than me gets paid.
2. To let everyone else do anything they like with the Stroke source code.
   That includes reusing it in their own free and paid projects, publishing
   bug fixes and improvements to Stroke, and building something substantially
   better on top of Stroke and charging for it.

There are no restrictions on how you use the source code. You can read it,
change it, build it, and run it yourself. The restrictions only apply to
publishing apps compiled from it.

Stroke is source-available, not open source. The Open Source Definition from
the Open Source Initiative does not allow conditions like the ones above, so
this is not an OSI-approved open source license.

Stroke is a paid app with a free trial. Buying Stroke Pro at
https://stroke.click buys a license key for the Stroke app. It does not change
these terms, and you do not need one to use the source code.

